      "target": "../sections/page-003.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n3\nAn Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\nResearch reported in the three articles that followãFoltz, Kintsch & Landauer (1998/this\nissue), Rehder, et al. (1998/this issue), and Wolfe, et al. (1998/this issue)ãexploits a new\ntheory of knowledge induction and representation (Landauer and Dumais, 1996, 1997) that\nprovides a method for determining the similarity of meaning of words and passages by\nanalysis of large text corpora. After processing a large sample of machine-readable\nlanguage, Latent Semantic Analysis (LSA) represents the words used in it,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Opening of the article introducing the three companion papers in the issue—Foltz, Kintsch, & Landauer (1998), Rehder et al. (1998), and Wolfe et al. (1998)—which build on Landauer and Dumais's (1996, 1997) theory of knowledge induction and representation. Explains that LSA represents words and word sets (sentences, paragraphs, essays) via singular value decomposition (SVD), a matrix decomposition technique akin to factor analysis applicable to large text corpora, and simulates human cognitive phenomena from recognition-vocabulary acquisition to word categorization, semantic priming, discourse comprehension, and essay-quality judgment. Introduces two ways to construe LSA, beginning with (1) a practical expedient for estimating contextual usage substitutability and meaning similarities among words and text segments.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
