      "pageEnd": 4,
      "target": "../sections/page-004.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n4\nwords and text segments that such relations may reflect, or (2) as a model of the\ncomputational processes and representations underlying substantial portions of the\nacquisition and utilization of knowledge. We next sketch both views.\nAs a practical method for the characterization of word meaning, we know that LSA\nproduces measures of word-word, word-passage and passage-passage relations that are\nwell correlated with several human cognitive phenomena involving association or semantic\nsimilarity.  Empirical evidence of this will be reviewed shortly. The",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues the two construals of LSA—as a practical method for characterizing word and passage meaning similarity and as a computational model of knowledge acquisition and utilization—and notes that LSA word–word, word–passage, and passage–passage measures correlate well with human cognitive phenomena, allowing objective prediction of word-based passage similarity in discourse processing. Emphasizes that LSA similarity estimates are not simple contiguity frequencies, co-occurrence counts, or usage correlations, but rely on mathematical analysis that infers deeper predictors of human meaning-based judgments than surface contingencies. Acknowledges that LSA induces representations from text alone without direct perceptual, instinctual, or embodied experience, yet still accesses verbal descriptions and outcomes of those processes to the extent writers express them in words.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
