      "target": "../sections/page-006.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n6\nAnother way to think of this is that LSA represents the meaning of a word as a kind\nof average of the meaning of all the passages in which it appears, and the meaning of a\npassage as a kind of average of the meaning of all the words it contains.  LSA's ability to\nsimultaneouslyãconjointlyãderive representations of these two interrelated kinds of\nmeaning depends on an aspect of its mathematical machinery that is its second important\nproperty.  LSA assumes that the choice of dimensionality in which all of the local word-\ncontext relations are simultaneo",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that LSA conjointly represents the meaning of a word as a kind of average of the passages in which it appears and the meaning of a passage as an average of the words it contains. Introduces LSA's second key property: dimensionality reduction from the number of initial contexts to a much smaller (though still large) optimal dimensionality that captures mutual implications of words and passages and improves approximations to human cognitive relations, analogous in spirit to psycholinguistic semantic features. Notes that LSA also applies a preprocessing step weighting each word's distribution across usage contexts, and introduces Landauer and Dumais's (1997) broader claim that LSA constitutes a fundamental computational theory of knowledge acquisition and induction.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
