      "target": "../sections/page-007.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n7\nthan appears to be available in experience, the infamous problem of the \"insufficiency of\nevidence\" or \"poverty of the stimulus.\" The LSA mechanism that solves the problem\nconsists simply of accommodating a very large number of local co-occurrence relations\n(between the right kinds of observational units) simultaneously in a space of the right\ndimensionality. Hypothetically, the optimal space for the reconstruction has the same\ndimensionality as the source that generates discourse, that is, the human speaker or writer's\nsemantic space. Naturally obser",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains how LSA addresses the \"insufficiency of evidence\" or \"poverty of the stimulus\" problem by accommodating a large number of local word–context co-occurrence relations simultaneously in a lower-dimensional space matching the dimensionality of the discourse source, using matrix decomposition to capture indirect constraints and mutual entailments latent in local observations. Summarizes three principal empirical findings supporting this claim—(1) LSA word-meaning similarities closely match human judgments, (2) LSA's rate of vocabulary acquisition from text approximates that of humans, and (3) these results depend strongly on representation dimensionality—and argues that while LSA's psychological reality remains an open empirical question, it cannot be dismissed a priori.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
