      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n8\nknowledge.)  Thus, we propose to researchers in discourse processing not only that they\nuse LSA to expedite their investigations, but that they join in the project of testing,\ndeveloping and exploring its fundamental theoretical implications and limits.\n  \nWhat is LSA? \nLSA is a fully automatic mathematical/statistical technique for extracting and inferring\nrelations of expected contextual usage of words in passages of discourse. It is not a\ntraditional natural language processing or artificial intelligence program; it uses no humanly\nconstructed dict",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Invites discourse-processing researchers both to use LSA practically and to test its theoretical implications and limits, then opens the \"What is LSA?\" section. Describes LSA as a fully automatic mathematical/statistical technique that uses no human-constructed dictionaries, knowledge bases, semantic networks, grammars, or syntactic parsers, taking only raw text segmented into unique word strings and meaningful passages. Outlines the three main steps: constructing a word-by-passage frequency matrix, applying a preliminary cell-weighting transformation reflecting word importance and information content across discourse, and decomposing the matrix via singular value decomposition (SVD) into row, column, and diagonal scaling matrices.",
