      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n17\n Additional detail about LSA \nAs mentioned, one additional part of the analysis, the data preprocessing transformation,\nneeds to be described more fully. Before the SVD is computed, it is customary in LSA to\nsubject the data in the raw word-by-context matrix to a two-part transformation. First, the\nword frequency (+ 1) in each cell  is converted to its log.  Second, the information-theoretic\nmeasure,    entropy  , of each word  is computed as -Ö p log p over all entries in its row, and\neach cell entry then divided by the row entropy value. The effect",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Details LSA's standard two-part data preprocessing transformation prior to SVD: converting each cell's word frequency (+1) to its logarithm, and dividing each cell entry by the word's row entropy (-sum p log p) so that words are weighted by their local importance within a passage and inversely by their overall cross-passage entropy (emphasizing specific meaning-bearing words). Points readers to mathematical and computational references (Berry, 1992; Bellcore LSI papers) and to the interactive web site http://LSA.colorado.edu/, which provides precomputed LSA spaces trained on encyclopedias, TASA schoolchild reading corpora, newspapers, introductory psychology textbooks, and a cardiac domain corpus.",
