      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n18\nand are necessarily noisy.  Therefore, studies using them require the use of replicate cases\nand statistical treatment in a manner similar to human data.\n \n\u0000\u0002\u0001\u0004\u0003\u0006\u0005\u0007\u0003\t\b\u0004\n\f\u000b\f\n\f\r\u000f\u000e\u0006\r\u000f\u0010\u0006\u0011\u0012\u0010\u0014\u0013\u0004\u0015\u0016\u000b\u0018\u0017\u0018\u0019\u0004\u001a\u001c\u001b\u001e\u001d \u001f\t\u0010\u001e\u001d\u0004!\"\u0015\"#\u0004\r$\u0019\u0004\u001b\u001e\u000b\u0018%\u0018\u001d\u0004\u0010\u001e&'\u000b\f\u0015\"\u0013\u0004(\u001e\u0015 \nHow well does LSA actually work as a representational model and measure of human\nverbal concepts? Its performance has been assessed more or less rigorously in several\nways. We give eight examples:\n(1) LSA was assessed as a predictor of query-document topic similarity judgments.\n(2) LSA was assessed as a simulation",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that LSA similarity values are sample-based and noisy—requiring replicate cases and statistical testing just like human behavioral data—and enumerates eight empirical domains in which LSA has been evaluated as a representational model and measure of human verbal concepts: (1) query–document topic similarity in information retrieval, (2) word–word relations and vocabulary synonym tests, (3) subject-matter multiple-choice tests, (4) text coherence and comprehension, (5) lexical priming experiments, (6) holistic grading of student essays, (7) matching instructional text to learners, and (8) lexical relations (synonyms, antonyms, singular–plural, compounds), word sorting, single-digit representations, and neuropsychological category clustering (Laham, 1997b), plus Kintsch's (1998) construction-integration modeling.",
