      "target": "../sections/page-019.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n19\nmetaphor and context effects in decision making. We will take space here to review only\nsome of the most systematic and pertinent of these results.\n LSA and information retrieval \nJ. R. Anderson (1990) has called attention to the analogy between information retrieval and\nhuman semantic memory processes. One way of expressing their commonality is to think\nof a searcher as having in mind a certain meaning, which he or she expresses in words, and\nthe system as trying to find a text with the same meaning. Success, then, depends on the\nsystem representing",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Opens the \"LSA and information retrieval\" section, noting J. R. Anderson's (1990) analogy between information retrieval and human semantic memory and explaining how Latent Semantic Indexing (LSI) outperforms literal term-matching systems by matching queries to documents of similar topical meaning even when they use different words. Describes representing a document database as a term-by-document matrix reduced via SVD to 50–400 dimensions—where document vectors are weighted averages of their word vectors and vice versa—and summarizes early benchmark evaluations against human relevance judgments across standard document collections.",
