      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n20\nperformance ranged from just equivalent to the best prior methods up to about 30% better.\nIn a recent project sponsored by the National Institute of Standards and Technology, LSI\nwas compared with a large number of other research prototypes and commercial retrieval\nschemes.  Direct quantitative comparisons among the many systems were somewhat\nmuddied by the use of varying amounts of preprocessingãthings like getting rid of\ntypographical errors, identifying proper nouns as special, differences in stop lists, and the\namount of tuning that systems were",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Concludes the information-retrieval review—noting that LSI improved retrieval by up to 30% on standard collections, achieved a 16% ceteris paribus improvement over standard vector retrieval without dimension reduction in NIST/TREC evaluations (Dumais, 1994), and succeeded in assigning manuscripts to reviewers (Dumais & Nielsen, 1992) and filtering research papers (Foltz & Dumais, 1992)—and opens \"LSA and synonym tests.\" Reports that in large-corpus LSA spaces, near-neighbor words generally share close semantic relations: dictionary synonym and antonym pairs both averaged cosines around .18 (over 12 times that of unrelated word pairs from the same set), with singular–plural pairs and analyzable compound–component pairs showing comparable or higher similarity.",
