      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n21\nNonetheless, the relationship between some close neighbors in LSA space can\n\u0006\u0015\u0003\u0004\u0003\u0002\u0001\u0002\u001e,#\n\u0006\u0015$\t\u0001\u0002\"4\"\n\u00161+\u0015\u0000\u0011%&\u0016\u0015\u001e-\b4\u0000\u0002\u000b!#\n\u0006\t\u0014\u0015\u001e\n\u0002!\u00005\f\n*\n\f\n7\u0007\r\n\u0001\n\u0000\u0002\u000b!+\t\u0001\u0002\"4\"\n\u0016\u0007\u000f\n\u0001\u0002$\u0015\u0012\n\r\u0019\u001e-\u0001\u0002\u0012\u0015\u0006\t%\n\u0001\u0004\u001e,\u0006\t\u0003\u0002\u0005\u0015#\n\u001e,%\n\u000f\n()#4\b\n\u00051\u0001\n\u0003\u0002\u0006\t\u001e,#\n$\u0015\u0000\u0011\u0006\u0015\u0017\n\f\n\u0000from the encyclopedia space), and some pairs that should be close are not.  It's impossible\nto say exactly why these oddities occur, but it is plausible that some words that have more\nthan one contextual meaning receive a sort of average high-dimensional placement that out\nof context signifies nothing, and that many words are sampled",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses why individual close neighbors in LSA space can occasionally appear anomalous or why expected neighbors can be missed—such as polysemous words receiving an averaged placement, sparse sampling in training corpora roughly equivalent in size to a 9th-grader's print exposure, and LSA's omission of syntax, logic, and pragmatics—cautioning researchers to evaluate LSA over averages of word or passage pairs rather than isolated pairs. Introduces the objective evaluation of LSA synonymy using 80 retired items from the ETS Test of English as a Foreign Language (TOEFL), trained on corpora such as Associated Press newswire text, Grolier's Academic American Encyclopedia, and the TASA schoolchild reading corpus (Footnote 2).",
