      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the TOEFL synonym experiment in which LSA was trained on the first ~150-word portions of 30,473 Grolier's Encyclopedia articles (4.5 million words, yielding vectors for 60,000 words) and chose the multiple-choice alternative with the highest cosine to the stem word. LSA scored 65% correct (guessing with probability .25 on six items with unseen words), identical to the average score of a large sample of non-native US college applicants; item-level correlations between LSA cosines and student choice proportions averaged r = .70 overall and r = .44 across incorrect alternatives, with LSA's errors often reflecting contextual/paradigmatic associations rather than contrastive syntactic or semantic distinctions. Footnote 2 concludes with details of the 11-million-word TASA corpus available on http://LSA.colorado.edu.",
