      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n25\nversus concrete similarity relations. Anglin measured the semantic similarity of every pair\nof words by the proportion of subjects who put them in the same pile. He found that parts\nof speech clustered moderately in both child and adult sets, and, confirming the hypothesis\nbehind the study, that adults showed more evidence of use of abstract categories than did\nchildren.\nLaham and Landauer measured the similarity between the same word pairs by\ncosines based on 5 grade-partitioned scalings of samples of schoolchild readingã3rd, 6th,\n9th, 12th grade  a",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reports Laham and Landauer's simulation of Anglin's (1970) word-sorting study using five grade-partitioned TASA corpus scalings (3rd, 6th, 9th, 12th grade, and college) across 20 words (190 pairs). Correlations between LSA cosines and human sorting proportions rose monotonically with corpus grade level, from r = .50 (children) and .35 (adults) at 3rd grade to r = .61 (children) and .50 (adults) at college level, and across levels of conceptual abstraction within part-of-speech classes reached r = .80–.90. However, LSA separated grammatical parts of speech much less sharply than humans did (within-class vs. between-class mean cosines of .15 vs. .13 at 3rd grade, rising to .23 vs. .17 at college level).",
