      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Introduction to Latent Semantic Analysis\n33\nconceptual continuum, such as level of sophistication or relevance to a particular topic, can\nbe measured.\n Summary and some caveats \nIt is clear enough from the conjunction of all these formal and informal results that LSA is\nable to capture and represent significant components of the lexical and passage meanings\nevinced in judgment and behavior by humans.  The following papers exploit this ability in\ninteresting and potentially useful ways that simultaneously provide additional\ndemonstrations and tests of the method and its underlying theory. Howev",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Completes the sentence on measuring texts' relative positions along a conceptual continuum (such as sophistication or topical relevance) and opens \"Summary and some caveats.\" Concludes that while LSA captures substantial components of human lexical and passage meaning, it lacks syntax, word-order sensitivity, logic, and non-linguistic perceptual/pragmatic experience, and often goes awry on individual word pairs or sentences even though it succeeds on averages across many items. Balances this caveat by arguing that LSA's broad empirical success across cognitive tasks should not be underestimated, as no prior theory in linguistics, psychology, or artificial intelligence has taken raw human-scale text input and produced veridical simulations across such a wide range of meaning-based judgments.",
